3.1 Different logics in programs
Logical arguments play by rules, which is what makes believe them. However, these rules change from context to context, much like the rules of a game. See for yourself.
If you wanted to explain the reasoning you could give further examples, for instance replace (Administrator, Add User, Remove User) with (Teacher, Teach, Grade) or (Chef, Cook, Clean). To emphasize the logic we might also begin to strip away artifacts of language and leave the main content. \[
\begin{array}{rcl}
\text{If Teacher} & \text{then} & \text{can Teach} \\
\text{If Teacher} & \text{then} & \text{can Grade} \\
\hline
\text{If Teacher} & \text{then} & \text{can Teach and Grade}
\end{array}
\qquad
\begin{array}{rl}
\text{Chef} & \to & \text{cook} \\
\text{Chef} & \to & \text{clean} \\
\hline
\text{Chef} & \to & \text{cook and clean}
\end{array}
\] where \(\to\) reads as “implies” or “if … then …”. Thinking like a mathematician you would soon give up on examples and replace their roles with variables \((A, B, C)\) and write the argument as \[
\tag{Rule of Implied Conjunction}
\begin{array}{rcl}
A & \to & B \\
A & \to & C \\
\hline
A & \to &  B \text{ and } C.
\end{array}
\] Congratulations, you have just found a rule of “formal logic”, formal here refers to “form”, i.e. shape of the argument, not that the argument needs cloth napkins.
Now let us keep the form (shape) of the argument but change the topic. Notice your belief in this form of reasoning quickly vanishes.
It is striking that most human reasoning knows intuitively not to use the form of argument we used on example 1 with example 2. We could replicate the same experiment and replace (USB port, Mouse, Keyboard) with (Egg, Cookies, Brownies) or variaables (A, B, C) and had we begun with this example we would have instead derived a fallacy instead of a rule! \[
\tag{Fallacy of Implied Conjunction}
\begin{array}{rcl}
A & \to & B \\
A & \to & C \\
\hline
A & \not\to &  B \text{ and } C.
\end{array}
\] Note that in notation, a “strike through” of a symbol means it is not true, so the fallacy is that \(A\) does not lead to \(B\) and \(C\).
So what should we believe as logical, the rule of conjunction or the fallacy of conjunction? Both are used correctly in everyday reasoning but they seem to say the exact opposite beliefs. This is the entire point of this article: to make a convincing argument is to play by rules within an agreed to context. Like the rules of a video game which are not always the same from logic to logic. Each logic is a universe of its own. Play with enough logics and you might be quicker to pick up rules of a new one and you will also start to appreciate what it is you really like about the rules of your favorite game, I mean, logic.
When you play a playground game it matters to agree on the rules otherwise you might make a splendid play only to have your opponent accuse you of cheating. It is the same with math and computation. Here is the command [2,3,5]+[2,4,8] given as input to two programming platforms. Surprise: the answers are different and each correct in some sense.

12.3 Calculus with Sums, Products, and Powers Copy
